      "text": "We begin by noticing that since all three roots, $r$, $s$, and $t$, satisfy the given equation, we can use Vieta's formulas to determine other expressions:\n1. The sum of the roots, $r + s + t$, is $20$.\n2. The sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time, $rs + rt + st$, is $18$.\nWork backwards from these values:\nFrom Vieta's formulas:\n\\begin{align*}\nr + s + t &= 20 \\\\\nrs + rt + st &= 18\n\\end{align*}\n\nThroughout the problem, we note that $1+r$ comes from factoring into $x - r$. Therefore, $(r + 1)(s + 1)(t+1)$ is the expansion of the product with all of the roots being one more than the roots of $x^3 - 20x^2 + 18x - 7$, each of which we can consider as a change in coefficient calculation factorize into a cube. Accordingly, expanding gives:\n\\begin{align*}\n(r + 1)(s + 1)(t+1) &= rsst(s+1)t+rsst+rt+ts{/eq}\n\nNow, we wish to calculate $(r + 1)(s + 1)(t+1)$. We observe that:\n\\begin{align*}\n(r + 1)(s + 1)(t+1) &= rs + rt + rs + r + st + s + r + st + st + rs \\\\\n&= rs + rt + rs + r + st + s + rt + st + st + rs \\\\\n&= 3(rs + rt + st) + 3(r + s + t) + 3 \\\\\n&= 3(18) + 3(20) + 3 \\\\\n&= 54 + 60 + 3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe now simply substitute this into the desired expression:\n\\begin{align*}\n(1+r)(1+s)(1+t) &= (r+1)(s+1)(t+1) \\\\\n&= 3(rs + rt + st) + 3(r + s + t) + 3 \\\\\n&= 3(18) + 3(20) + 3 \\\\\n&= 54 + 60 + 3 \\\\\n&= \\boxed{117}.\n\\end{align*} The final answer is 117",
